<DocsLayout title="Webhooks" activeNav="/platform/integrations/webhooks">
  <h1>Webhooks</h1>
  <p>
    A webhook endpoint receives platform events over HTTP: builds being created and finishing,
    artifacts arriving, identifiers and credentials changing, team membership moving. Deliveries are
    signed, retried, and recorded, so this screen tells you not only what you subscribed to but
    whether it actually arrived.
  </p>

  <Screenshot
    src="integrations-webhooks"
    alt="Webhooks tab showing one endpoint with its subscribed events and a delivery history including one exhausted delivery"
    caption="An endpoint, its subscriptions, and the recent deliveries underneath."
  />

  <h2>The endpoint row</h2>
  <p>
    Under the name and URL sit the two facts you check first: the <strong>secret hint</strong>, which
    identifies which signing secret is live without revealing it, and <strong>last delivery</strong>.
    The event pills below list the subscription, and the line under them says whether filters narrow
    it — <code>No filters · all matching events are delivered</code> means every subscribed event in
    the workspace goes to this endpoint.
  </p>
  <p>
    Deliveries are signed with HMAC-SHA256 and retried automatically, as the header on the screen
    states. Verification is covered in the <a href="/guides/webhooks">Webhooks guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Creating an endpoint</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>New endpoint</strong> needs a name, a URL, and at least one event. The event list is a
    fixed catalog, and it is worth reading rather than guessing at names:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Group</th><th>Events</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Builds</td>
        <td><code>build.created</code>, <code>build.started</code>, <code>build.cancel_requested</code>, <code>build.completed</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Artifacts and files</td>
        <td><code>artifact.created</code>, <code>artifact.deleted</code>, <code>app_file.created</code>, <code>app_file.deleted</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Applications</td>
        <td><code>mobile_app.created</code>, <code>mobile_app.updated</code>, <code>mobile_app.deleted</code>, and the same three for <code>app_identifier</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Credentials</td>
        <td><code>credential.created</code>, <code>credential.updated</code>, <code>credential.deleted</code>, the expiry warnings, and <code>credential.verification_failed</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Team</td>
        <td><code>team.invitation.created</code>, <code>team.invitation.accepted</code>, <code>team.invitation.revoked</code>, <code>team.member.role_changed</code>, <code>team.member.removed</code></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    <strong>There is no <code>build.failed</code>.</strong> A finished build reports through
    <code>build.completed</code> and carries its own status, so a receiver that cares only about
    failures should subscribe to <code>build.completed</code> and check the status in the payload.
  </p>
  <p>
    Credential events are only offered when the workspace's credential API profile exposes secret
    material. If your list is missing them, that setting is why.
  </p>

  <h3>Filters</h3>
  <p>
    Without filters an endpoint receives every subscribed event in the workspace. Filters narrow it by
    application, build status, build tag, and profile type — which is how you keep one team's release
    channel from hearing about every other app's nightly builds.
  </p>

  <h2>Testing and rotating</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Send test</strong> delivers a synthetic event so you can verify a receiver before a real
    build depends on it. The test appears in the delivery list like any other, which makes the result
    unambiguous: nothing listed means the problem is on this side, a failed row means it is at your
    receiver.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Rotate secret</strong> issues a new signing secret and shows it once. Rotation takes
    effect immediately, so deploy the new secret to your receiver first if you cannot tolerate a
    window of rejected deliveries — the retry schedule will cover a short gap, but not a long one.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading deliveries</h2>
  <p>
    The delivery table shows the event, the platform's verdict, the number of attempts, the receiver's
    HTTP status with the request duration, and when it happened.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Status</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>pending</code></td><td>Queued, not yet attempted</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>delivering</code></td><td>In flight</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>retry_scheduled</code></td><td>Failed, and another attempt is scheduled</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>delivered</code></td><td>Accepted by the receiver</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>exhausted</code></td><td>Every retry failed. This one needs your attention.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    In the screenshot the <code>build.created</code> delivery is <code>exhausted</code> after five
    attempts against a receiver answering <code>500</code>. Opening a delivery shows the payload that
    was sent, the response preview, and the last error, which is usually enough to tell a receiver bug
    from a signature mismatch.
  </p>
  <p>
    A delivery can be replayed after you fix the receiver. A replay is recorded as a new delivery that
    references the original, so the failed attempt stays in the history instead of being overwritten.
  </p>

  <h2>Watching endpoint health</h2>
  <p>
    Each endpoint tracks consecutive failures. That counter is the thing to alert on rather than
    individual deliveries — a single failure is noise, ten in a row is an outage:
  </p>
  <pre><code>appmctl --json webhooks list |
  jq -e '[.data[] | select(.consecutive_failures &gt; 3)] | length == 0'</code></pre>

  <h2>Doing the same from the CLI</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/guides/cli/integrations/webhooks"><code>appmctl webhooks</code></a> covers the whole
    lifecycle: create with filters, update, test, rotate, list deliveries, and redeliver.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
